Manage Docker containers — the running instances of images. These are the most frequently used 
Docker commands.

Create and start a new container from a Docker image
#docker run <image_name>

Run a container in detached background mode
#docker run -d <image_name>

Run a container interactively with a bash shell
#docker run -it <image_name> /bin/bash

Create a container with a custom name
#docker run --name <container_name> <image_name>

Map host port 8080 to container port 80
#docker run -p 8080:80 <image_name>

Mount a host directory into the container as a volume
#docker run -v <host_path>:<container_path> <image_name>

Automatically remove the container after it exits
#docker run --rm <image_name>

Set environment variables inside the container
#docker run -e <variable_name>=<value> <image_name>

Connect the container to a specific Docker network
#docker run --network <network_name> <image_name>

Limit container memory usage to 512 MB
#docker run --memory 512m <image_name>

Limit the container to use 1.5 CPU cores
#docker run --cpus 1.5 <image_name>

Start a stopped container
#docker start <container_name_or_id>

Gracefully stop a running container
#docker stop <container_name_or_id>

Restart a container
#docker restart <container_name_or_id>

Forcefully stop a running container immediately
#docker kill <container_name_or_id>

Pause all running processes inside a container
#docker pause <container_name_or_id>

Resume a paused container
#docker unpause <container_name_or_id>

Remove a stopped container
#docker rm <container_name_or_id>

Forcefully remove a running container
#docker rm -f <container_name_or_id>

List all running containers
#docker ps

List all containers including stopped containers
#docker ps -a

Display only container IDs
#docker ps -q

Open a bash shell inside a running container
#docker exec -it <container_name_or_id> bash

Execute a command inside a running container
#docker exec <container_name_or_id> <command>

View logs generated by a container
#docker logs <container_name_or_id>

View live container logs in real time
#docker logs -f <container_name_or_id>

Display the last 100 lines of container logs
#docker logs --tail 100 <container_name_or_id>

Display detailed metadata of a container in JSON format
#docker inspect <container_name_or_id>

Show real time CPU memory and network usage statistics of containers
#docker stats

Display running processes inside a container
#docker top <container_name_or_id>

Show filesystem changes made inside a container
#docker diff <container_name_or_id>

Copy files from container to host machine
#docker cp <container_name_or_id>:<container_path> <host_path>

Copy files from host machine to container
#docker cp <host_path> <container_name_or_id>:<container_path>

Rename a container
#docker rename <old_container_name> <new_container_name>

Wait until a container stops and display its exit code
#docker wait <container_name_or_id>

Update resource limits of a running container
#docker update --memory 1g <container_name_or_id>

Display port mappings of a container
#docker port <container_name_or_id>

Attach terminal input output to a running container
#docker attach <container_name_or_id>

Create a new Docker image from a container state
#docker commit <container_name_or_id> <new_image_name>

Export a container filesystem as a tar archive
#docker export <container_name_or_id> > <file_name>.tar

